#. Tag: title
#: using-layers.docbook:2
#, no-c-format
msgid "Layers"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:4
#, no-c-format
msgid "This chapter gives an overview of how layers work in &krita;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-layers.docbook:9
#, no-c-format
msgid "Background information on layers"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:11
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Extensive use of &krita; will almost require you to have some knowledge of "
"layers. Using layers, you can work on one part of the image without touching "
"the rest of it, and most effects are best applied on a layer, instead of on "
"the whole image. Of course, if you do want to apply an effect to an entire "
"image, &krita; does offer you that possibility, and there is nothing against "
"it."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:18
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The idea behind layers is quite simple. As the name suggests, layers lie on "
"top of each other, and together form the layer stack. The final resulting "
"image is that what you see when looking through the stack from top to "
"bottom. This means that usually the upper layers of your image will have "
"more or less transparency, since you cannot look through a layer which has "
"no transparency. (&krita; works with opaqueness instead of transparency. A "
"layer that is 100 percent opaque is 0 percent transparent, and vice versa.) "
"A layer higher in the stack gets applied later than one lower in the stack. "
"For example, if your image contains four layers, numbered from 1 (lowest) to "
"4 (highest), the effect that layer number 4 adds to the image, is applied to "
"the result from applying layers 1 through 3."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:30
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Every image you edit in &krita; contains layers. When you create a new "
"image, the layer box (usually shown at the bottom right of your screen, see "
"<link linkend=\"commands-palettes-layers-layers\">this section</link>) will "
"contain one layer. The painting and editing you do is then applied to that "
"layer. Once you add more layers, you can choose on which part of the image "
"you want to work, by selecting the respective layer. All further painting is "
"then applied to that layer, until you select another one."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:38
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Layers are also an excellent way to check whether adding certain effects (or "
"applying certain image modifications) come out right. Add a layer which "
"contains what you want to try out, and show or hide it with the eye icon in "
"the layer box. You can especially profit from this method if you have "
"multiple effects to check out: show and hide them in any combination, and "
"decide which you like best. And since you can move the layers around, you "
"can also experiment with the order in which the effects are applied."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:46
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"See the <link linkend=\"tutorial-select-layer\">Selections and layers "
"tutorial</link> for a small hands-on introduction."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-layers.docbook:53
#, no-c-format
msgid "The layer box"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:55
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The layer box is the instrument you will use most to work with layers. It "
"gives an overview of the layers that are present in your image, and using it "
"you can manage layers by adding, removing, reordering or modifying them."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:58
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The layer box consists of three parts. The middle part gives an overview of "
"the layers in the image. At the top, you can set some properties for the "
"current layer. At the bottom, a couple of layer management options can be "
"found. The next sections describe these three parts in more detail."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-layers.docbook:66
#, no-c-format
msgid "Layer overview"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:68
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This part shows you which layers are present in your image. In a tree-like "
"structure, the layer group hierarchy is shown: layers that are contained "
"within a layer group are displayed a bit to the right to indicate their "
"belonging to that group."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:72
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For each layer, a thumbnail preview and its name are shown. The layer name "
"is preceded by a folder icon if it is a group layer. Furthermore, two "
"indicators are present: the eye icon shows whether the layer is currently "
"visible (an open eye indicates that the layer is visible, a closed eye "
"indicates that it is not), and the lock icon shows whether the layer is "
"locked. No changes can be made to a locked layer."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:79
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you click on a layer's eye icon, its visibility is switched from on to "
"off or vice versa. Clicking on the lock icon enables or disables editing of "
"that layer. You can click on the name of the current layer to rename it. "
"Note that to rename a layer, it has to be the current one. You do not need "
"to activate a layer in order to make it (in)visible or (un)locked via the "
"eye and lock icons, respectively: these work directly."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:86
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Doubleclick on a layer entry in the list to open the <link linkend="
"\"commands-dialogs-layers-layerproperties\"><guilabel>Layer Properties</"
"guilabel></link> dialog. This dialog shows a layer's colorspace and profile. "
"You can also change its name, opacity and composite mode here."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-layers.docbook:95
#, no-c-format
msgid "Layer options"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:96
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The top of the layer box contains two controls for setting properties of the "
"currently selected layer. The list box at the left allows you to quickly set "
"the layer's composite mode. The spin field and slider at the right can be "
"used to change the layer's opacity."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:101
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"At the bottom of the layer box, there are five buttons. From left to right, "
"these are as follows. The <guibutton>New Layer</guibutton> icon brings up a "
"submenu from which you can choose which type of layer you want to add. This "
"menu can also be opened by clicking with the &RMB; on the layer box. The "
"<guibutton>Move Layer Down</guibutton> and <guibutton>Move Layer Up</"
"guibutton> buttons move the current layer one level down and up, "
"respectively, within the current layer group. If the layer is already the "
"last or first within the layer group, trying to move it further will move it "
"out of the layer group. The <guibutton>Layer Properties</guibutton> button "
"opens the <link linkend=\"commands-dialogs-layers-layerproperties"
"\"><guilabel>Layer Properties</guilabel></link> dialog, just as when you "
"would have doubleclicked on the layer. The <guibutton>Delete Layer</"
"guibutton> button deletes the current layer."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-layers.docbook:121
#, no-c-format
msgid "Working with layers"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:123
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Because layers are quite important when extensively using &krita;, you can "
"perform a lot of operations on them. These are all available via the <link "
"linkend=\"commands-menus-layer\"><guimenu>Layer</guimenu> menu</link>. Some "
"of the possibilities:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-layers.docbook:131
#, no-c-format
msgid "Add, remove, and duplicate layers;"
